---
import GuideLayout from '@layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import ReferenceBlock from '@components/ReferenceBlock.astro';
import { config } from './config';
import { toolPath } from '@lib/paths';
import { toolPathBySlug } from '@lib/tools/tool-link';
import { categories } from '@data/categories';
import type { EcosystemEntry } from '@data/types';

interface Props {
  title: string;
  description: string;
  readMinutes: number;
  updatedAt: string;
  entry: EcosystemEntry;
}

const props = Astro.props;
const category = categories.find(c => c.slug === config.categorySlug)!;
const toolHref = toolPath({ slug: config.slug, segment: category.segment });
const contrastHref = toolPathBySlug('color-contrast-checker');
const shadesHref = toolPathBySlug('color-shades-generator');
---

<GuideLayout
  {...props}
  toolSlug={config.slug}
  toolName={config.name}
  toolCategorySlug={config.categorySlug}
>
  <section id="quick-answer">
    <h2>How do you check a palette for colorblindness? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Paste the colors, with a space between each one. The page draws that row as you typed it,
      then draws the same row seven more times: protanopia, deuteranopia, tritanopia, the three
      anomaly types, and achromatopsia. Each row is labeled. You do not flip a menu to see the
      next type, which is how a pass on one deficiency hides a fail on another.
    </p>
    <p>
      A colorblind simulator here is a picture of your hex values, not an upload and not a vision
      test. This colorblind palette checker is a CVD simulator built on the Machado color vision
      simulation. For example, try #d32f2f #388e3c #1976d2. The red and the green land close together
      under deuteranopia. The blue stays apart. The note under the rows names that pair and the
      type. Copy keeps the hex list and that sentence.
    </p>
    <a href={toolHref} class="cta-link">Open the Colorblind Simulator →</a>
  </section>

  <section id="types">
    <h2>What do protanopia, deuteranopia, and tritanopia mean? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Which cone type protanopia, deuteranopia, and tritanopia each remove is the whole label.
      Cone cells come in three kinds. L cones respond to longer wavelengths, M cones to the middle,
      S cones to shorter wavelengths. Protanopia is the simulation with no L cones. Deuteranopia
      is the simulation with no M cones. Tritanopia is the simulation with no S cones. The first
      two are the common red-green deficiencies. Tritanopia is rare. The label on the row is the
      type you are looking at, so a yellow swatch is not left to guesswork.
    </p>
    <p>
      Protanomaly, deuteranomaly, and tritanomaly are the shifted-cone versions, not the missing-cone
      versions. This page uses severity 0.6 from the same published table, which is a milder move
      than severity 1.0. Anomalous trichromacy in a real eye can sit anywhere on that scale. One
      step is enough to see whether a palette is fragile. It is not a measurement of a person.
    </p>
    <p>
      Achromatopsia is the row with no hue. Each color becomes a gray from its relative luminance,
      using the sRGB coefficients 0.2126, 0.7152, and 0.0722 on linear light. Two colors of similar
      lightness become similar grays even when a cone simulation still separates them. That row is
      there so you can see it. The collapse note does not use it, because it would name grayscale
      for almost every pair and hide the cone type that actually failed.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="key-idea" heading="The model is Machado 2009">
      <p>
        Gustavo M. Machado, Manuel M. Oliveira, and Leandro A. F. Fernandes published the matrices
        in IEEE TVCG, 2009. How it works: the page multiplies linear sRGB by the severity 1.0 matrix for each
        dichromacy and by the severity 0.6 matrix for each anomaly. A neutral gray stays near gray,
        because each matrix row sums to about 1.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="lightness">
    <h2>Why do pure red and pure green stay apart? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Why two mid reds and greens can collapse while pure red and pure green do not is a lightness
      fact, not a proverb. People say red and green look the same under deuteranopia. Pure red #ff0000 and pure green
      #00ff00 do not, in this model. Green is much lighter than red, and the simulation keeps that
      lightness. The swatches move, and they remain easy to tell apart. A proverb is a bad test
      color.
    </p>
    <p>
      The pairs that collapse are the ones that already share a lightness. #d32f2f and #388e3c are
      a mid red and a mid green. Under deuteranopia they land close. Under protanopia they move
      less. Compare protanopia and deuteranopia on the same colors: both rows stay visible. A deuteranopia simulator and a protanopia color check are both on the page because
      those two shifts are not the same shift. If you only viewed one, the other could still fail.
    </p>
    <p>
      Blue against yellow is the tritanopia question. #1565c0 and #f9a825 stay far apart under the
      red-green rows. Read the tritanopia row for that pair rather than assuming every bright pair
      is a red-green problem. Both distances in the note are Euclidean distance in 8-bit sRGB.
      One is the pair as typed. The other is the pair after the simulation. Neither is a uniform delta E.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="note">
    <h2>When does the collapse note appear? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Read the note that names a collapsed pair before you change a token. Protanopia versus
      deuteranopia, and dichromacy versus anomalous trichromacy, are separate rows of the same
      Machado color vision simulation. The note is the check after the picture. It looks at every pair that started at least 40
      apart in sRGB. If a cone simulation brings that pair under 36, the note names the type, both
      hex values, and the two distances. The closest landing wins when more than one type fails.
      The sentence is specific: under deuteranopia, #d32f2f and #388e3c, with the numbers the model
      just produced.
    </p>
    <p>
      The note stays hidden when you typed one color, when two swatches were already the same, and
      when every pair that started distinct stays distinct. A quiet page is a palette that survived
      the six cone simulations. It is not a promise about achromatopsia, and it is not a WCAG grade.
      Contrast of text on a background is a different question. Open the
      <a href={contrastHref}>contrast checker</a> for the pair you will actually ship.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="note" heading="What the note will not do">
      <p>
        It will not invent a failure for a pair the matrices still separate. Pure red and pure
        green are the example. If the note is hidden, look at the rows anyway. A shift can be
        obvious and still above the collapse line.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="limits">
    <h2>What does this page refuse to pretend? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      It does not diagnose color vision. A simulation shows a palette to someone with typical
      cones, as a model of someone else. It does not sort people into types, and the anomaly rows
      are one severity from a table, not a threshold from a clinic. About 8 percent of men and
      about 0.5 percent of women have a red-green deficiency. Those rates are why the check is
      worth doing. They are not a result this page computes.
    </p>
    <p>
      Blue cone monochromacy is not simulated. It is not a matrix in the Machado table this page
      uses, and a made-up matrix would be a confident wrong picture. Image upload is also out.
      A palette of hex values is the input a token file already has, and it never leaves the
      browser. If the colors live in a screenshot, read the hex out first.
    </p>
    <p>
      Build the ramp in the <a href={shadesHref}>color shades generator</a>, then paste the stops
      that carry meaning: the brand, the success color, the danger color. Eight colors is the
      cap on this page, which is enough for a status set and too small for a whole theme. That
      limit keeps every simulation on one screen.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Checking a single type and shipping">
      <p>
        Deuteranopia can pass a pair that protanopia fails, and the reverse happens too. Leave
        the rows in view. A menu that shows one type at a time is how the failing pair gets missed.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Using the page as an eye test">
      <p>
        The swatches are a model of colors, not a test of a visitor. Someone who wants to know
        their own vision needs a proper examination. This page will not tell them.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="next">
    <h2>What should you open next? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Take the pair the note named into the contrast checker and read the ratio on the real
      background. A pair can stay distinct under deuteranopia and still fail 4.5:1 as text on
      white. The two checks answer different questions, and a palette needs both.
    </p>
    <p>
      If a teammate sent hsl() or a CSS name, convert it first, then come back with hex. The
      simulator accepts those forms, and the copy block writes hex so the next tool sees one
      spelling. The arithmetic stays on the device either way.
    </p>
    <a href={toolHref} class="cta-link">Check #d32f2f against #388e3c →</a>
  </section>
</GuideLayout>

<style>
  .cta-link {
    display: inline-flex;
    margin-top: var(--space-3);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-medium);
  }
</style>
